  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on comparing the skewness of the lotteries by assessing which has a greater probability assigned to favorable outcomes. This would involve noting that B has twice the chance of yielding a positive outcome (0.2% vs. 0.1%) and thus, under the reason's definition, is more left-skewed and preferred. However, the individual in the verbal report does not consider the higher probability of winning in B. Instead, the justification focuses solely on the higher potential gain in A. The information considered by the individual (magnitude of the best outcome) differs from that required by the reason (probability of favorable outcomes). Furthermore, the individual’s justification (favoring higher payoff) contradicts the rule of the decision reason, which would favor B due to its higher win probability. There is no evidence that the individual assessed the distribution of outcomes in terms of skewness or favorability frequency. Therefore, it is unlikely the reason was used.",
