  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on assessing which lottery concentrates more probability on favorable outcomes (i.e., more left-skewed). This involves comparing the distribution of probabilities across outcomes with respect to favorability. The individual does consider the probabilities of loss and the size of the loss, which are components of the lotteries, but does not evaluate the overall skewness or make a judgment based on the concentration of probability on favorable outcomes. Instead, the reasoning reflects a trade-off between probability and outcome magnitude—common in expected utility or heuristic reasoning—but not skewness. There is no indication that the individual structured their thinking around the asymmetry or skew of the distributions. Therefore, while the relevant data (probabilities and outcomes) are partially considered, the conceptual framework of skewness is absent from the justification. The reasoning pattern does not align with the decision rule of preferring the more left-skewed lottery.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

